In this lesson we'll look at variables and formulas, and why they're so useful in everyday maths. A variable is just a letter, like x or n, that stands in for a number we don't know yet, or a number that might change. Think of it as a little placeholder waiting for a real value to slot in. Next, many everyday rules can be turned into a short formula, which is really just a recipe linking two or more variables together. For example, the cost of apples is fifty cents times the number of apples you buy, so the total cost equals fifty times n. The perimeter of a square is four times the side length, so perimeter equals four times s. Even converting Celsius to Fahrenheit follows a rule, where Fahrenheit equals one point eight times Celsius plus thirty-two. Once you know what the letters stand for, you simply substitute, meaning you swap the letter for the actual number and crunch it. So if the side of a square is six, perimeter equals four times six, which is twenty-four. Formulas are short, reusable, and they make patterns visible, so one neat line replaces a whole sentence. Now let's see it in action.